-- A browser's subscription to push messages.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand, like
-- `0013_sour_apocalypse.sql`, `0014_branch_geolocation.sql` and
-- `0015_consent_uniqueness.sql`.
--
-- ## What this closes
--
-- A push message needs somewhere to go. This is that somewhere: one row per
-- browser registration, with the endpoint the push service will accept a
-- delivery on, the browser's public key, and its auth secret.
--
-- ## Why `endpoint` is unique
--
-- A browser rotates its `p256dh` and `auth` for the same endpoint. Registering
-- again is therefore not a duplicate: it is the same registration with newer
-- keys. The unique index is what makes that one row instead of two, and the
-- upsert is what makes it keep the new keys.
--
-- The uniqueness is on the endpoint alone, and not on
-- `(business_id, customer_id, endpoint)`: the endpoint identifies the
-- registration everywhere, and a browser that signs out and is used by a
-- different customer has to move, not accumulate.
--
-- ## Why `business_id` is denormalised next to `customer_id`
--
-- So a tenant-scoped query never needs a join to prove ownership. The RLS
-- predicate filters directly on it, and a delivery list is built by business
-- rather than by customer.
--
-- ## Why the endpoint is a capability
--
-- It is. Anyone holding it can push to that browser. It is stored here because
-- it has to be, and it is treated as a secret everywhere else: it never
-- appears in a log, and `message_log` keeps only a SHA-256 digest of it in
-- `push_endpoint_hash`, which is enough to answer a support question and
-- useless to anyone who reads the row.
--
-- ## What this migration does NOT do
--
-- **It does not enable row-level security.** `push_subscriptions` is listed in
-- `BUSINESS_ID_TABLES`, so it receives a policy when the separate, reviewed
-- RLS rollout runs. That rollout is this table's isolation story and it must
-- not arrive by accident in a `CREATE TABLE`.
--
-- **It does not store a customer's position.** A subscription is a browser, not
-- a place. Where a customer is, is a question the venue's own position
-- answers, and the position of the device that received a message is not
-- recorded anywhere.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
CREATE TABLE "push_subscriptions" (
	"id" uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() NOT NULL,
	"business_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"customer_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"endpoint" text NOT NULL,
	"p256dh" text NOT NULL,
	"auth" text NOT NULL,
	"user_agent" text,
	"created_at" timestamp with time zone DEFAULT now() NOT NULL,
	"last_seen_at" timestamp with time zone
);
